 "intro": "Near Mount Callinicus, by Larissa in Thessaly, 171 BC, at the start of the Third Macedonian War. You are the consul Publius Licinius Crassus. King Perseus’s cavalry and light troops have come to your camp to offer battle.",
 "debrief": "In Livy’s account, the battle was fought by cavalry and light troops while the Roman legions stayed in camp. Perseus’s Thracian infantry and Macedonian horse routed the Roman and allied cavalry, and the Romans lost about 2,000 foot and 200 horse. Perseus did not bring up his phalanx to attack the camp, and the Romans withdrew across the Peneus. Perseus offered peace, but Rome refused to treat after a defeat, and won at Pydna in 168 BC. The other choices are what-ifs.",
